{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-11790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(6,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,4),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "086103fc78e786756d3edac1958c0bcab145462cb875a38575747d73c8d01993"} {"question" "q-11790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}=-6y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=-9y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=3y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=6y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=9y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=9y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "086103fc78e786756d3edac1958c0bcab145462cb875a38575747d73c8d01993"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+1)^{2}=-2(y+4)"] ["math" "(x+1)^{2}=-4(y+4)"] ["math" "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)"] ["math" "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+4)"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}=2(y+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-8),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "086103fc78e786756d3edac1958c0bcab145462cb875a38575747d73c8d01993"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)"] ["math" "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/assets/2819ceb4214f8df19ef349794c243771a1080bccf0bf2bbdbdbca9be67d4fbdb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (4+4)^{2} & =4p(-6-6) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "086103fc78e786756d3edac1958c0bcab145462cb875a38575747d73c8d01993"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)"] ["math" "(x-6)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y+4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/state.json" "file_sha256" "0353f450ac354c56610a18a9093fa4587a9352e72e64fc88705a7e2982fdd2c9"} {"question" "q-11837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/